7. Karuppa Gounder v. Palaniammal
, a Bench of the Madras High Court was dealing with a case where the premium amounts for the policy were paid out of moneys which the assured had received from the joint family. It was held that the funds for the insurance policy did not come from the joint family and that the policy belongs to the individual member and not to the joint family. Srinivasan, J., dealing with the observations of their Lordships of the Supreme Court in , confined them to the facts of that case. At p. 91 (of Mad LJ)= (at p. 248 of AIR) he stated;
"But where a coparcener has effected insurance upon his own life, though he might have received the premia from out of the funds which he might have received from the joint family. It does not follow that the joint family insured the life of the members or paid the premia in relation thereto. It is undeniable that a member of a coparcenery may with the moneys which he might receive from the coparcenery effect an insurance upon his own life for the benefit of the members of his immediate family.
